  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2z+9}{3}\\ge -z-1.$\n\nThe solution is $z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$",
